This works, but building your SQL via raw string concatenation quickly becomes hard to read and maintain (missing spaces, hard to add dynamic clauses, etc.). Consider one of the following improvements:

1. Use MyBatis’s @Select or @SelectProvider on the mapper method instead of returning a plain string:
   @Select("SELECT gt.group_id AS groupId, ... FROM ${GROUP_TABLE_NAME} gt JOIN ${MetalakeMetaMapper.TABLE_NAME} mt ON gt.metalake_id = mt.metalake_id WHERE mt.metalake_name = #{metalakeName} AND gt.deleted_at = 0")
   List<GroupPO> listGroupPOsByMetalake(@Param("metalakeName") String metalakeName);

2. Leverage the MyBatis SQL Builder DSL (org.apache.ibatis.jdbc.SQL) to assemble your query in a structured way:
   public String listGroupPOsByMetalake(Map<String, Object> params) {
     return new SQL() {{
       SELECT("gt.group_id AS groupId, gt.group_name AS groupName, ...");
       FROM(GROUP_TABLE_NAME + " gt");
       JOIN(MetalakeMetaMapper.TABLE_NAME + " mt ON gt.metalake_id = mt.metalake_id");
       WHERE("mt.metalake_name = #{metalakeName}");
       WHERE("gt.deleted_at = 0");
     }}.toString();
   }

