It’s great that you added a call to scx_central__destroy(skel) in the default/error path to avoid leaking your BPF skeleton—but I’d recommend a couple of improvements:

1. Centralize cleanup: rather than sprinkling destroy calls around each error branch, consider a single “cleanup” label at the end of main. Use goto cleanup; from each error path so all teardown logic lives in one place.

2. Check for NULL: if skel failed to initialize, scx_central__destroy(skel) may crash. Either verify skel is non-NULL before destroying or only call destroy after a successful init.

3. Ensure exit status: after destroying the skeleton you should still return a non-zero exit code (or call exit(EXIT_FAILURE)) to signal the error.

Putting it all together, your main might look like:

    int ret = EXIT_SUCCESS;
    struct scx_central_bpf *skel = scx_central__open_and_load(...);
    if (!skel) {
        fprintf(stderr, "failed to open BPF skeleton\n");
        ret = EXIT_FAILURE;
        goto cleanup;
    }

    switch (some_option) {
        ...
        default:
            fprintf(stderr, help_fmt, basename(argv[0]));
            ret = EXIT_FAILURE;
            goto cleanup;
    }

cleanup:
    if (skel)
        scx_central__destroy(skel);
    return ret;

