The removed BITOP macro had a couple of pitfalls around operator precedence, argument evaluation, and safety:

1. Operator Precedence:  
   In C, << binds more tightly than most binary operators, so writing
     a[idx] op 1 << shift
   actually parses as
     a[idx] op (1 << shift)
   but it can be unclear when op is something like |= or &=. Always wrap your shifts in parentheses:
     a[idx] op (1UL << (b % (8 * sizeof(size_t))))

2. Parenthesize Macro Arguments:  
   If b is an expression, you need parentheses around it in both the division and the modulo:
     (size_t)(b) / (8 * sizeof(size_t))
     (size_t)(b) % (8 * sizeof(size_t))

3. Cast and Literal Suffixes:  
