The change to `(tools ??= []).Add(functions[i]);` looks like it’s trying to lazily initialize a list, but introduces a couple of issues:

1. You’re mutating a local `tools` variable rather than the `options.Tools` property that the rest of the method expects, so nothing is actually being added to `options.Tools`.  
2. The inline `??=` inside the `Add` call can be hard to read and debug.

I’d suggest one of these clearer alternatives:

```csharp
// Option A: Initialize up front
options.Tools ??= new List<Function>();
options.Tools.Add(functions[i]);

// Option B: Assignment & add on separate lines
if (options.Tools == null)
    options.Tools = new List<Function>();
options.Tools.Add(functions[i]);
```

This makes it explicit that you’re working on `options.Tools`, ensures it’s never null, and improves overall readability.

