This new `GetIconByTheme` method is a good addition for theme-aware icon handling. The logic for retrieving the dark theme icon, including the fallback to `DefaultDarkPackageIconSource`, looks solid.

However, there's a critical issue: the method currently only defines a return path for the `RestoreApplicationIconTheme.Dark` case. If the `theme` parameter is any other value, the method will not return a value, which will result in a compile-time error (CS0161: 'YourNamespace.YourClass.GetIconByTheme(RestoreApplicationIconTheme)': not all code paths return a value).

To fix this, you'll need to ensure all possible code paths return a `BitmapImage`. Consider:
1.  Adding `else if` blocks for other themes (e.g., `Light`).
2.  Providing a default `BitmapImage` to return at the end of the method if no specific theme matches.
3.  Throwing an `ArgumentOutOfRangeException` if an unsupported `theme` value is passed, though returning a default icon is often preferred for UI robustness.

A `switch` statement might also be more readable and maintainable if you plan to support several distinct themes:

```csharp
private BitmapImage GetIconByTheme(RestoreApplicationIconTheme theme)
{
    switch (theme)
    {
        case RestoreApplicationIconTheme.Dark:
            // Your existing Dark theme logic
            if (_package.DarkThemeIcon == null)
            {
                return DefaultDarkPackageIconSource;
            }
            var darkThemeBitmapImage = new BitmapImage();
            darkThemeBitmapImage.SetSource(_package.DarkThemeIcon);
            return darkThemeBitmapImage;

        case RestoreApplicationIconTheme.Light: // Example
