The new GetIconByTheme method correctly returns a dark‑theme icon or falls back to the default, but it has a few gaps and opportunities for cleanup:

1. Missing Light (and other) theme handling. Right now if theme != Dark you end up returning null. Consider using a switch (or switch expression) so you explicitly cover Dark, Light, and/or a default case, and throw or log if an unsupported theme is passed.

2. Duplication and instantiation cost. You’re creating a new BitmapImage on every call, even if the same package icon is requested repeatedly. If icons are immutable you could cache the resulting BitmapImage per theme (lazy‑loaded) to avoid unnecessary allocations.

3. Asynchronous SetSource. Depending on your framework (UWP vs WPF) you may need SetSourceAsync or BeginInit/EndInit to properly load from a stream without blocking the UI thread.

4. Null checks and error handling. You guard against _package.DarkThemeIcon being null, but you may also want to validate that the stream is readable or wrap SetSource in try/catch to surface I/O failures.

